INSERT INTO workspace.fizzco.runbook_chunks (chunk_id, doc_id, title, feed, doc_type, section, position, n_tokens, text) VALUES
  ('CHK-CUTOVER-001#c1', 'CHK-CUTOVER-001', 'Cutover checklist - retire the legacy warehouse', 'all', 'checklist', 'Cutover checklist: retire the legacy warehouse', 1, 200, '# Cutover checklist: retire the legacy warehouse ## Before cutover (five working days ahead) - [ ] All four feeds (sell_in, sell_out, inventory, promo) have a reconciliation with status MATCH for the latest month end. A MISMATCH that has a MATCH rerun counts as closed. - [ ] The object inventory has been validated with its five rules: all template columns present, unique object ids (format `OBJ-###`), every dependency exists, object type is Pipeline, Notebook or Table, and status is Active or Retired. - [ ] The storage container name is `fizzco-landing` in every pipeline, notebook and configuration file. The container `fizzco-raw-archive` is archive only and keeps its name. - [ ] The lineage documents are current and mention the archive step for inventory. - [ ] The runbooks for sell_in, sell_out, inventory and promo, and the general first-response runbook, have been reviewed in the last 30 days. - [ ] Row-level access is tested: an East manager sees only East data and a West manager sees only West data. ## Cutover day - [ ] Freeze changes to the pipelines. - [ ] Run the daily load for each feed with the load_date set explicitly. The parameter has no'),
  ('CHK-CUTOVER-001#c2', 'CHK-CUTOVER-001', 'Cutover checklist - retire the legacy warehouse', 'all', 'checklist', 'Before cutover (five working days ahead)', 2, 195, 'East data and a West manager sees only West data. ## Cutover day - [ ] Freeze changes to the pipelines. - [ ] Run the daily load for each feed with the load_date set explicitly. The parameter has no default value. - [ ] Run the reconciliation for each feed and record the job id and reconciliation id. - [ ] Do not proceed if any feed shows MISMATCH. Use the runbook for that feed, rerun, and reconcile again. - [ ] Confirm that the archive step for inventory finished and that the archive folder holds the landing files. ## After cutover (first working day) - [ ] Compare the first gold tables with the legacy reports for one region and one week. - [ ] Keep the legacy warehouse read-only for 30 days before it is switched off. - [ ] Record the outcome and any open risks in the migration log. ## Rollback If a Sev 1 problem is found on cutover day, stop the loads, keep the legacy warehouse as the source of truth and raise a ticket in the data-engineering queue. Nothing is deleted from the legacy warehouse during cutover.'),
  ('DD-GOLD-001#c1', 'DD-GOLD-001', 'Data dictionary - gold tables', 'all', 'dictionary', 'Data dictionary: gold tables', 1, 200, '# Data dictionary: gold tables All tables are in the catalog `workspace`, schema `fizzco`. All data is synthetic and covers the first quarter of 2026. ## gold_sell_in_vs_sell_out Grain: one row per week, retailer and SKU. Compares what Fizzco shipped to retailers (sell-in) with what retailers sold to shoppers (sell-out). Columns: `week_start` (the Monday that starts the sales week), `region` (East or West), `retailer_id`, `sku`, `sell_in_units`, `sell_in_gross_amount`, `sell_in_discount_amount`, `sell_in_net_amount` (gross minus discount), `sell_out_units`, `sell_out_amount` (value at shelf price), `variance_units` (sell-in units minus sell-out units) and `variance_pct`. The table has 68 rows. ## gold_recon_results Grain: one row per reconciliation run. Columns: `recon_id` (format RECON-####), `job_id` (format JOB-####), `feed` (sell_in, sell_out, inventory or promo), `recon_date` (inclusive cut-off date), `source_rows`, `trusted_rows`, `diff_rows` (source minus trusted), `status` (MATCH or MISMATCH) and `note`. The table has 13 rows and includes the 31 March 2026 sell_in mismatch and its rerun. ## gold_promo_performance Grain: one row per promotion. Columns: `promo_id` (format PROMO-2026-####), `sku`, `retailer_id`, `region`, `start_date`, `end_date`, `discount_pct` (5, 10, 15 or 20), `promo_days`, `baseline_units`, `promo_units`, `incremental_units`, `uplift_pct` and `promo_cost`. The table has 12 rows. ## dim_retailer One row per retailer. Columns: `retailer_id` (R001 to R010), `retailer_name`, `region` (East or West) and `channel` (Supermarket, Convenience or Discount). The'),
  ('DD-GOLD-001#c2', 'DD-GOLD-001', 'Data dictionary - gold tables', 'all', 'dictionary', 'gold_promo_performance', 2, 99, '(5, 10, 15 or 20), `promo_days`, `baseline_units`, `promo_units`, `incremental_units`, `uplift_pct` and `promo_cost`. The table has 12 rows. ## dim_retailer One row per retailer. Columns: `retailer_id` (R001 to R010), `retailer_name`, `region` (East or West) and `channel` (Supermarket, Convenience or Discount). The table has 10 rows. ## dim_product One row per product. Columns: `sku` (for example FZ-COLA-330), `product_name`, `category` (Beverage or Snack), `pack_size` and `list_price`. The table has 8 rows. ## Trusted tables The trusted layer holds cleaned copies of the raw feeds: `trusted_sell_in` (adds `net_amount`), `trusted_sell_out` (sale_date, retailer_id, sku, units, sale_amount), `trusted_inventory` (snapshot_date, dc_id, sku, stock_on_hand) and `trusted_promo` (adds `promo_days`).'),
  ('INC-2026-03-31-001#c1', 'INC-2026-03-31-001', 'Incident note - sell_in reconciliation mismatch on 31 March 2026', 'sell_in', 'incident', 'Incident note: sell_in reconciliation mismatch on 31 March 2026', 1, 200, '# Incident note: sell_in reconciliation mismatch on 31 March 2026 ## Summary On 31 March 2026 the quarter-end reconciliation for the sell_in feed (job JOB-0049, reconciliation RECON-0009) reported MISMATCH. The source had 63 rows, the trusted table was counted as 60, and the difference was 3 rows. No data was lost. The cause was an off-by-one error in the date filter used to count trusted rows: the end date of the window was excluded. The three rows dated 2026-03-31 were in the trusted table but were not counted. The rerun, job JOB-0053 with reconciliation RECON-0013, showed 63 and 63 with status MATCH. ## Timeline | Step | What happened | |---|---| | Reconciliation run | JOB-0049 compared raw and trusted sell_in rows from 2026-01-01 to 2026-03-31 and reported source 63, trusted 60, diff 3, MISMATCH (RECON-0009) | | Same-day checks | The other three feeds reconciled with MATCH on the same date: sell_out JOB-0050 (127 rows), inventory JOB-0051 (40 rows), promo JOB-0052 (12 rows) | | Diagnosis | Raw and trusted files both held 63 rows. The two counting functions in `recon_utility.py` treated the end date differently: the source count used `<=` and the trusted count used `<` | |'),
  ('INC-2026-03-31-001#c2', 'INC-2026-03-31-001', 'Incident note - sell_in reconciliation mismatch on 31 March 2026', 'sell_in', 'incident', 'Timeline', 2, 200, 'promo JOB-0052 (12 rows) | | Diagnosis | Raw and trusted files both held 63 rows. The two counting functions in `recon_utility.py` treated the end date differently: the source count used `<=` and the trusted count used `<` | | Fix | One character changed: `<` to `<=` in `count_trusted_rows` | | Rerun | JOB-0053 reran the sell_in reconciliation and reported MATCH with 63 and 63 (RECON-0013), note "rerun after the end-date fix" | ## Root cause The trusted-row counter filtered with `d < end` while the source-row counter filtered with `d <= end`. A reconciliation window is inclusive of its end date, so rows dated on the last day were dropped from the trusted count only. The pattern is easy to spot: the missing rows all carry the last day of the window, and the number of missing rows equals the number of rows on that day. ## How it was verified Four tests were written first. On the old code 3 of the 4 failed; after the one-character fix all 4 passed. One of the tests runs the reconciliation on the real raw and trusted files and expects MATCH with 63 and 63. Another test confirms that a'),
  ('INC-2026-03-31-001#c3', 'INC-2026-03-31-001', 'Incident note - sell_in reconciliation mismatch on 31 March 2026', 'sell_in', 'incident', 'How it was verified', 3, 120, 'old code 3 of the 4 failed; after the one-character fix all 4 passed. One of the tests runs the reconciliation on the real raw and trusted files and expects MATCH with 63 and 63. Another test confirms that a genuinely missing row still produces a MISMATCH, so the fix does not hide real data loss. ## What to do if it happens again If a reconciliation shows a small MISMATCH on a month-end or quarter-end date, count the rows dated on the last day of the window in both raw and trusted. If that number equals the difference, suspect the end-date filter before suspecting data loss. Follow the sell_in runbook and use the reconciliation policy for the rerun rules.'),
  ('REG-Q1-2026-001#c1', 'REG-Q1-2026-001', 'Job and reconciliation register - Q1 2026', 'all', 'register', 'Job and reconciliation register: Q1 2026', 1, 200, '# Job and reconciliation register: Q1 2026 ## How to read this register Each line is one reconciliation run. A reconciliation run is executed by a job (format `JOB-####`) and produces a result (format `RECON-####`). Row counts are cumulative from 1 January 2026 up to and including the cut-off date. Status is MATCH when diff_rows is 0 and MISMATCH otherwise. There were 13 runs in the quarter, 12 MATCH and 1 MISMATCH. The one MISMATCH was RECON-0009 (job JOB-0049, sell_in, 31 March 2026); its rerun RECON-0013 (job JOB-0053) was MATCH. ## Register by cut-off date ### January month end (2026-01-31) - RECON-0001 is job JOB-0041, feed sell_in, cut-off 2026-01-31: source 24 rows, trusted 24 rows, difference 0, status MATCH. - RECON-0002 is job JOB-0042, feed sell_out, cut-off 2026-01-31: source 48 rows, trusted 48 rows, difference 0, status MATCH. - RECON-0003 is job JOB-0043, feed inventory, cut-off 2026-01-31: source 10 rows, trusted 10 rows, difference 0, status MATCH. - RECON-0004 is job JOB-0044, feed promo, cut-off 2026-01-31: source 8 rows, trusted 8 rows, difference 0, status MATCH. ### February month end (2026-02-28) - RECON-0005 is job JOB-0045, feed sell_in, cut-off 2026-02-28: source 38 rows, trusted 38 rows, difference 0, status MATCH.'),
  ('REG-Q1-2026-001#c2', 'REG-Q1-2026-001', 'Job and reconciliation register - Q1 2026', 'all', 'register', 'January month end (2026-01-31)', 2, 200, 'job JOB-0044, feed promo, cut-off 2026-01-31: source 8 rows, trusted 8 rows, difference 0, status MATCH. ### February month end (2026-02-28) - RECON-0005 is job JOB-0045, feed sell_in, cut-off 2026-02-28: source 38 rows, trusted 38 rows, difference 0, status MATCH. - RECON-0006 is job JOB-0046, feed sell_out, cut-off 2026-02-28: source 78 rows, trusted 78 rows, difference 0, status MATCH. - RECON-0007 is job JOB-0047, feed inventory, cut-off 2026-02-28: source 29 rows, trusted 29 rows, difference 0, status MATCH. - RECON-0008 is job JOB-0048, feed promo, cut-off 2026-02-28: source 12 rows, trusted 12 rows, difference 0, status MATCH. ### March quarter end (2026-03-31) - RECON-0009 is job JOB-0049, feed sell_in, cut-off 2026-03-31: source 63 rows, trusted 60 rows, difference 3, status MISMATCH. Note: date filter excluded the end date; 3 rows dated 2026-03-31 not counted. - RECON-0010 is job JOB-0050, feed sell_out, cut-off 2026-03-31: source 127 rows, trusted 127 rows, difference 0, status MATCH. - RECON-0011 is job JOB-0051, feed inventory, cut-off 2026-03-31: source 40 rows, trusted 40 rows, difference 0, status MATCH. - RECON-0012 is job JOB-0052, feed promo, cut-off 2026-03-31: source 12 rows, trusted 12 rows, difference 0, status MATCH. - RECON-0013 is job JOB-0053, feed sell_in, cut-off 2026-03-31:'),
  ('REG-Q1-2026-001#c3', 'REG-Q1-2026-001', 'Job and reconciliation register - Q1 2026', 'all', 'register', 'March quarter end (2026-03-31)', 3, 110, 'cut-off 2026-03-31: source 40 rows, trusted 40 rows, difference 0, status MATCH. - RECON-0012 is job JOB-0052, feed promo, cut-off 2026-03-31: source 12 rows, trusted 12 rows, difference 0, status MATCH. - RECON-0013 is job JOB-0053, feed sell_in, cut-off 2026-03-31: source 63 rows, trusted 63 rows, difference 0, status MATCH. Note: rerun after the end-date fix. ## Quick facts - The first job of the quarter is JOB-0041 and the last is JOB-0053. - Each month end has one reconciliation per feed: sell_in, sell_out, inventory and promo, run in that order. - The only rerun in Q1 2026 is JOB-0053 (RECON-0013), which re-reconciled sell_in for 2026-03-31 after the end-date fix.'),
  ('GLO-KPI-001#c1', 'GLO-KPI-001', 'KPI glossary', 'all', 'glossary', 'KPI glossary', 1, 200, '# KPI glossary ## Sell-in and sell-out **Sell-in** is what Fizzco shipped and invoiced to retailers. **Sell-out** is what retailers sold to shoppers, measured from scan data. Sell-in is recorded in the sell_in feed and sell-out in the sell_out feed. A week starts on Monday, and the column `week_start` holds that Monday. ## Net sell-in sales Net sell-in sales is the invoiced value to retailers after discounts: gross amount minus discount amount. In the gold table it is the column `sell_in_net_amount`; in the trusted sell_in table it is `net_amount`. Always say "net sell-in sales". The phrase "net sales" alone is ambiguous. ## Why "net sales" is ambiguous Some legacy tables have a column called `net_sales` that holds shopper sales (what retailers sold to shoppers at shelf price), which is sell-out value and not Fizzco''s net sales. Fizzco''s definition of net sales is the value invoiced to retailers after discounts. A question about net sales must be answered with the invoiced value, not with the column that happens to be called net_sales. Do not compare the two values with each other as if they were on the same price basis. ## Shopper sales Shopper sales is the value of units that'),
  ('GLO-KPI-001#c2', 'GLO-KPI-001', 'KPI glossary', 'all', 'glossary', 'Why "net sales" is ambiguous', 2, 200, 'value, not with the column that happens to be called net_sales. Do not compare the two values with each other as if they were on the same price basis. ## Shopper sales Shopper sales is the value of units that retailers sold to shoppers at shelf price. In the gold table it is `sell_out_amount`. ## Variance Two kinds of variance are used. **Unit variance** (`variance_units`) is sell-in units minus sell-out units; positive means more units were shipped than sold. **Variance percentage** (`variance_pct`) is unit variance as a percentage of sell-in units; it is empty when there was no sell-in in the week. When a total is needed across many rows, divide the summed unit variance by the summed sell-in units; do not average the row percentages. ## Sell-through rate Sell-through rate is total sell-out units divided by total sell-in units, as a percentage with one decimal. A higher rate means shelves are emptying faster than they are being filled. ## Promotion uplift and cost **Baseline units** are the units expected over the promotion period without the promotion. **Incremental units** are promo units minus baseline units. **Uplift percentage** (`uplift_pct`) is incremental units as a percentage of baseline units. **Promo cost** is'),
  ('GLO-KPI-001#c3', 'GLO-KPI-001', 'KPI glossary', 'all', 'glossary', 'Promotion uplift and cost', 3, 110, 'Promotion uplift and cost **Baseline units** are the units expected over the promotion period without the promotion. **Incremental units** are promo units minus baseline units. **Uplift percentage** (`uplift_pct`) is incremental units as a percentage of baseline units. **Promo cost** is promo units multiplied by list price multiplied by the discount percentage divided by 100. **Promo cost per extra unit** is promo cost divided by incremental units. ## Reconciliation terms **Source rows** are the rows in the raw data for the window; **trusted rows** are the rows in the trusted table for the same window; **difference** is source minus trusted. Status is **MATCH** when the difference is zero and **MISMATCH** otherwise.'),
  ('HB-MIGRATION-001#c1', 'HB-MIGRATION-001', 'Migration operations handbook', 'all', 'handbook', 'Migration operations handbook', 1, 200, '# Migration operations handbook ## 1. Architecture in one page Fizzco is retiring its legacy warehouse and moving four feeds into a lake with three layers. Files arrive from retailers and internal systems by SFTP. A pipeline copies each file into the landing container `fizzco-landing`, in a folder named `raw/<feed>/`. This is the raw layer: files exactly as received. A Databricks notebook then reads the raw files, applies the cleaning rules and writes the trusted tables: `trusted_sell_in`, `trusted_sell_out`, `trusted_inventory` and `trusted_promo`. Gold tables are built on top of the trusted tables for reporting: `gold_sell_in_vs_sell_out`, `gold_promo_performance` and `gold_recon_results`. Analysts and managers read only the gold layer. Data engineers work in all three layers. The four feeds are sell_in (orders invoiced to retailers), sell_out (retailer scan data), inventory (distribution-centre stock snapshots) and promo (promotions). The container name `fizzco-landing` was previously `fizzco-raw`; the container `fizzco-raw-archive` is a different container that holds archived files only. ## 2. Naming conventions Pipelines are named `pl_fizzco_<feed>_load`, for example `pl_fizzco_promo_load`. Notebooks are stored under `/Fizzco/` and named `<feed>_transform`. Identifiers follow fixed formats: objects `OBJ-###`, jobs `JOB-####`, reconciliations `RECON-####`, promotions `PROMO-2026-####`, retailers `R###`. Regions are always East or West. Code and documents use the exact feed names sell_in, sell_out,'),
  ('HB-MIGRATION-001#c2', 'HB-MIGRATION-001', 'Migration operations handbook', 'all', 'handbook', '2. Naming conventions', 2, 200, 'example `pl_fizzco_promo_load`. Notebooks are stored under `/Fizzco/` and named `<feed>_transform`. Identifiers follow fixed formats: objects `OBJ-###`, jobs `JOB-####`, reconciliations `RECON-####`, promotions `PROMO-2026-####`, retailers `R###`. Regions are always East or West. Code and documents use the exact feed names sell_in, sell_out, inventory and promo, with the underscore. ## 3. The daily run Each feed has one file per load date, named `<feed>_<load_date>.csv`, with the date written as YYYY-MM-DD. The daily run processes the feeds in a fixed order: sell_in, sell_out, inventory, promo. The same order is used for reconciliation, so reconciliation ids for one cut-off date run in that order. The load date is always passed to the pipeline as the parameter `load_date`; the parameter has no default. Every run produces a job id. The job id is the key that links a pipeline run to its reconciliation result. When a support engineer reports a failure, the first question is always "what is the job id and the load date?" ## 4. Reject files When a notebook cannot accept a row, it does not stop the whole load. It writes the row to a reject file for that feed and load date and carries on. Rejected rows are therefore a normal'),
  ('HB-MIGRATION-001#c3', 'HB-MIGRATION-001', 'Migration operations handbook', 'all', 'handbook', '4. Reject files', 3, 200, '4. Reject files When a notebook cannot accept a row, it does not stop the whole load. It writes the row to a reject file for that feed and load date and carries on. Rejected rows are therefore a normal reason for the trusted table to hold fewer rows than the raw file. Every reject must be explained in the reconciliation note. The feed owners are: the sell-in owner for sell_in, the retailer owner for sell_out, the warehouse team for inventory and the commercial team for promo. A reject file is always sent to the feed owner, never fixed by hand in the trusted table. ## 5. Access and roles Data engineers can trigger and rerun pipelines and can read every layer. Analysts can read the gold tables and ask questions through the analytics assistant. Regional managers see only their own region: an East manager sees East rows and a West manager sees West rows. This is enforced by a row filter on the gold tables, not by separate copies of the data. Nobody edits trusted or gold tables by hand; corrections are made at the source and the pipeline is rerun. ## 6. Reruns in practice A rerun uses'),
  ('HB-MIGRATION-001#c4', 'HB-MIGRATION-001', 'Migration operations handbook', 'all', 'handbook', '5. Access and roles', 4, 200, 'row filter on the gold tables, not by separate copies of the data. Nobody edits trusted or gold tables by hand; corrections are made at the source and the pipeline is rerun. ## 6. Reruns in practice A rerun uses the same load date as the failed run and replaces the trusted rows for that date, so it is safe to repeat. A rerun is a new job with a new job id. After every rerun the reconciliation is run again and the result is recorded with a new reconciliation id and a note. The old failed result stays in the history. A failure is closed only when a rerun reconciles with MATCH. ## 7. Frequently asked questions **What is a MATCH?** A reconciliation where the source rows and the trusted rows for the window are equal, so the difference is zero. **What is a MISMATCH?** Any difference at all between source and trusted rows. There is no tolerance. **A reconciliation shows a difference of three rows on a month end. Where do I look first?** Count the rows dated on the last day of the window in raw and in trusted. If the count equals the difference, suspect the'),
  ('HB-MIGRATION-001#c5', 'HB-MIGRATION-001', 'Migration operations handbook', 'all', 'handbook', '7. Frequently asked questions', 5, 200, 'reconciliation shows a difference of three rows on a month end. Where do I look first?** Count the rows dated on the last day of the window in raw and in trusted. If the count equals the difference, suspect the end-date filter. **Is it safe to rerun a pipeline?** Yes. A rerun replaces the trusted rows for the load date and never appends duplicates. **Which container holds archived inventory files?** `fizzco-raw-archive`, in the folder `inventory/`. Only the inventory pipeline has an archive step. **Who owns promotion data?** The commercial team. Copy the promotion owner on any ticket about a promo reject. **Where do I find the history of a reconciliation?** In the reconciliation results table `gold_recon_results` and in the Q1 2026 job register. **What does the load_date look like?** A date in the format YYYY-MM-DD, for example 2026-03-31. **Do the pipelines retry on their own?** No. There is no automatic retry, no timeout policy and no failure alert. A failed run waits for a person. **Which regions exist?** East and West. ## 8. Known gaps The pipelines have no retry or timeout settings and no alert on failure, and the trusted target is not recorded in the pipeline definition. The'),
  ('HB-MIGRATION-001#c6', 'HB-MIGRATION-001', 'Migration operations handbook', 'all', 'handbook', '7. Frequently asked questions', 6, 70, 'run waits for a person. **Which regions exist?** East and West. ## 8. Known gaps The pipelines have no retry or timeout settings and no alert on failure, and the trusted target is not recorded in the pipeline definition. The container and notebook paths are fixed in the pipeline definitions rather than passed as parameters. These gaps are listed in the lineage documents and are accepted for the migration period.'),
  ('POL-RECON-001#c1', 'POL-RECON-001', 'Reconciliation policy', 'all', 'policy', 'Reconciliation policy', 1, 200, '# Reconciliation policy ## Purpose Reconciliation proves that nothing was lost between the raw and trusted layers. It compares the number of rows in the raw data with the number of rows in the trusted table for the same feed and the same date window. This policy applies to all four feeds: sell_in, sell_out, inventory and promo. ## Rules 1. **Window.** The window starts on 1 January 2026 and ends on the cut-off date. The cut-off date is inclusive: rows dated on the cut-off date are counted on both sides. 2. **Cut-off dates.** A reconciliation is run at every month end (31 January, 28 February, 31 March) and after every rerun. 3. **Tolerance.** The accepted difference is zero rows. A difference of one row or more is a MISMATCH, however small. 4. **Result record.** Every run writes one line to the reconciliation results with a reconciliation id (format `RECON-####`), the job id (format `JOB-####`), the feed, the cut-off date, the row counts, the difference, the status and a note. 5. **Notes.** The note is mandatory for every MISMATCH and for every rerun. It must say what caused the difference or why the rerun was needed. 6. **Reruns.** A rerun is'),
  ('POL-RECON-001#c2', 'POL-RECON-001', 'Reconciliation policy', 'all', 'policy', 'Rules', 2, 200, 'row counts, the difference, the status and a note. 5. **Notes.** The note is mandatory for every MISMATCH and for every rerun. It must say what caused the difference or why the rerun was needed. 6. **Reruns.** A rerun is a new job with a new job id and a new reconciliation id. The old result is never edited or deleted, so the history shows both the failure and the fix. 7. **No cosmetic fixes.** Never delete or add rows in the trusted table only to make the counts agree. 8. **Sign-off.** A MISMATCH is closed only when a rerun shows MATCH. The data owner signs off the closure. ## Investigating a MISMATCH Work through the checks in this order, stopping at the first one that explains the difference: 1. **Date window.** Count the rows dated on the cut-off date in raw and in trusted. If that number equals the difference, the end-date filter is the suspect. This was the cause of RECON-0009 on 31 March 2026. 2. **Duplicates.** Look for duplicate keys in the raw data that the transform collapsed. Document the number in the note. 3. **Rejects.** Count the rows in the reject file for the load. Rejected'),
  ('POL-RECON-001#c3', 'POL-RECON-001', 'Reconciliation policy', 'all', 'policy', 'Investigating a MISMATCH', 3, 117, 'cause of RECON-0009 on 31 March 2026. 2. **Duplicates.** Look for duplicate keys in the raw data that the transform collapsed. Document the number in the note. 3. **Rejects.** Count the rows in the reject file for the load. Rejected rows explain a lower trusted count and must be reported to the feed owner. 4. **Missing source rows.** Compare the file names and row counts of the source files with the raw layer. ## Reporting The reconciliation results feed the migration health view. A feed is healthy for a cut-off date when its latest reconciliation for that date is MATCH. A quarter is clean when every feed is healthy at the last cut-off date of the quarter.'),
  ('RB-GENERAL-001#c1', 'RB-GENERAL-001', 'Runbook - first response to any pipeline failure', 'all', 'runbook', 'Runbook: first response to any pipeline failure', 1, 200, '# Runbook: first response to any pipeline failure ## When to use this runbook Use this runbook when a Fizzco job fails and you do not yet know which feed is affected, or when the feed-specific runbook does not explain the failure. Each feed has its own runbook for sell_in, sell_out, inventory and promo; use the feed runbook as soon as you know the feed. ## First five minutes 1. Write down the job id (format `JOB-####`), the pipeline name (format `pl_fizzco_<feed>_load`) and the load_date. 2. Find the failed activity in the pipeline monitor. Pipelines have a copy activity first and a notebook activity second. Inventory has a third activity, the archive step. 3. Check whether the load_date parameter was set. It has no default value, so a manual run without it fails at once. 4. Check whether the same failure happened on the previous day. 5. Do not edit trusted or gold tables by hand. Do not delete files from `fizzco-landing`. ## Retry and timeout behaviour The pipelines have no automatic retry and no timeout policy. A failed run stays failed until someone reruns it. There is no failure alert either, so a failure is found by looking at'),
  ('RB-GENERAL-001#c2', 'RB-GENERAL-001', 'Runbook - first response to any pipeline failure', 'all', 'runbook', 'First five minutes', 2, 200, 'from `fizzco-landing`. ## Retry and timeout behaviour The pipelines have no automatic retry and no timeout policy. A failed run stays failed until someone reruns it. There is no failure alert either, so a failure is found by looking at the pipeline monitor or by the reconciliation. This is a known gap and is listed in the lineage documents under risks. ## Severity | Severity | Meaning | Example | |---|---|---| | Sev 1 | Blocks cutover or leaves a feed without data for more than one day | Two feeds failed on the cutover day | | Sev 2 | One feed late or a reconciliation MISMATCH that is not yet explained | JOB-0049, sell_in MISMATCH on 31 March | | Sev 3 | A cosmetic or archive-only problem; the trusted data is correct | Archive step failed after a good transform | ## Escalation Raise a ticket in the data-engineering queue. Include the job id, the pipeline name, the load_date, the failed activity, the severity and the reconciliation output. ## After the fix Rerun, run the reconciliation, and write a short note in the `note` column of the reconciliation result. Close the ticket only when the reconciliation shows'),
  ('RB-GENERAL-001#c3', 'RB-GENERAL-001', 'Runbook - first response to any pipeline failure', 'all', 'runbook', 'Escalation', 3, 41, 'the load_date, the failed activity, the severity and the reconciliation output. ## After the fix Rerun, run the reconciliation, and write a short note in the `note` column of the reconciliation result. Close the ticket only when the reconciliation shows MATCH.'),
  ('RB-INVENTORY-001#c1', 'RB-INVENTORY-001', 'Runbook - inventory load job failure', 'inventory', 'runbook', 'Runbook: inventory load job failure', 1, 200, '# Runbook: inventory load job failure ## Purpose and schedule This runbook covers failures of the pipeline `pl_fizzco_inventory_load`, which loads distribution-centre stock snapshots. The pipeline has three activities in order: `Copy_inventory_to_raw` copies the snapshot file into the `fizzco-landing` container (folder `raw/inventory/`); `Run_inventory_transform` runs the notebook `/Fizzco/inventory_transform` to build `trusted_inventory` (columns: snapshot_date, dc_id, sku, stock_on_hand); and `Archive_Landing_Files` copies the landing files to the archive container `fizzco-raw-archive` (folder `inventory/`). Inventory is the only feed with the archive step. The pipeline takes the parameter `load_date`. ## Symptoms An inventory job failure shows up as a Failed run, as a reconciliation MISMATCH, or as a stock_on_hand value that looks wrong for a distribution centre. ## Triage steps 1. Note the job id (format `JOB-####`) and the load_date. 2. Open the run and find which of the three activities failed. The recovery is different for each one. 3. If the first activity failed, check that the snapshot file for the load_date exists in the SFTP folder. 4. If the second activity failed, read the notebook error. Most failures are an unknown dc_id or an unknown sku. 5. If only the third activity, `Archive_Landing_Files`, failed, the trusted data is already correct. Do not rerun the whole'),
  ('RB-INVENTORY-001#c2', 'RB-INVENTORY-001', 'Runbook - inventory load job failure', 'inventory', 'runbook', 'Triage steps', 2, 200, 'folder. 4. If the second activity failed, read the notebook error. Most failures are an unknown dc_id or an unknown sku. 5. If only the third activity, `Archive_Landing_Files`, failed, the trusted data is already correct. Do not rerun the whole pipeline. ## Common causes specific to inventory - **Archive step failed after a successful transform.** The trusted table is complete and the reconciliation is MATCH, but the landing files were not archived. Rerun only the archive activity. Do not delete anything from `fizzco-landing` until the archive copy is confirmed. Be careful with container names: `fizzco-raw-archive` is the archive container; `fizzco-landing` is the landing container. They are different containers and must not be swapped. - **Missing snapshot.** One distribution centre did not send its snapshot. The trusted table then has fewer rows than expected for that snapshot_date. Ask the warehouse team to resend it. - **Unknown dc_id or sku.** The transform rejects rows whose dc_id or sku is not in the reference data. Fix the reference data and rerun. - **Negative stock_on_hand.** A negative value is rejected. Ask the warehouse team to confirm the count. ## Fix and rerun For a failure in the copy or transform activity, fix the cause'),
  ('RB-INVENTORY-001#c3', 'RB-INVENTORY-001', 'Runbook - inventory load job failure', 'inventory', 'runbook', 'Common causes specific to inventory', 3, 122, 'reference data. Fix the reference data and rerun. - **Negative stock_on_hand.** A negative value is rejected. Ask the warehouse team to confirm the count. ## Fix and rerun For a failure in the copy or transform activity, fix the cause and rerun the pipeline with the same load_date; the trusted rows for that snapshot_date are replaced. For a failure in the archive activity only, rerun the archive activity alone. ## Verify Run the reconciliation. Expect MATCH. Then confirm that the archive folder `inventory/` in `fizzco-raw-archive` holds the file for the load_date. ## Escalation Raise a ticket in the data-engineering queue with the job id, the load_date and the failed activity name. Mention whether the archive step is the only one that failed.'),
  ('RB-PROMO-001#c1', 'RB-PROMO-001', 'Runbook - promo load job failure', 'promo', 'runbook', 'Runbook: promo load job failure', 1, 200, '# Runbook: promo load job failure ## Purpose and schedule This runbook covers failures of the pipeline `pl_fizzco_promo_load`, which loads promotions. The pipeline copies the promotions file into the `fizzco-landing` container (folder `raw/promo/`) and runs the notebook `/Fizzco/promo_transform` to build `trusted_promo`. The trusted table adds one column, `promo_days`, which is end_date minus start_date. Promotion ids have the format `PROMO-2026-####`. The pipeline takes the parameter `load_date`. ## Symptoms A promo job failure shows up as a Failed run, as a reconciliation MISMATCH, or as a promotion that is missing from the promotion performance report. ## Triage steps 1. Note the job id (format `JOB-####`) and the load_date. 2. Open the failed activity. `Copy_promo_to_raw` failing means a source file problem; `Run_promo_transform` failing means a notebook problem. 3. Open the reject file written by the transform. Promo rejects are almost always a data-quality problem in the promotion itself, not a pipeline fault. 4. If both activities succeeded, run the reconciliation. ## Common causes specific to promo - **End date before start date.** A promotion whose end_date is earlier than its start_date cannot have a valid promo_days and is rejected. Ask the promotion owner to correct the dates. - **Invalid discount.** The discount_pct must'),
  ('RB-PROMO-001#c2', 'RB-PROMO-001', 'Runbook - promo load job failure', 'promo', 'runbook', 'Common causes specific to promo', 2, 200, 'specific to promo - **End date before start date.** A promotion whose end_date is earlier than its start_date cannot have a valid promo_days and is rejected. Ask the promotion owner to correct the dates. - **Invalid discount.** The discount_pct must be 5, 10, 15 or 20. Any other value is rejected. - **Unknown retailer or sku.** The retailer_id (R001 to R010) or sku is not in the dimensions. Fix the dimension or the promotion, then rerun. - **Duplicate promo_id.** Two rows with the same promo_id are treated as one promotion; the later row wins and the earlier row is written to the reject file. - **Overlapping promotions.** Two promotions for the same sku and retailer that overlap in time are loaded, but the promotion performance gold table will double count baseline units. Flag overlaps to the commercial team. ## Fix and rerun Correct the source promotion or the dimension, then rerun the pipeline. A promo rerun replaces all trusted promotions for the load_date. Each rerun gets a new job id and reconciliation id. ## Verify Run the reconciliation and expect MATCH. Then check that the promotion appears in `gold_promo_performance` with a non-null uplift_pct. ## Escalation Raise a ticket in the'),
  ('RB-PROMO-001#c3', 'RB-PROMO-001', 'Runbook - promo load job failure', 'promo', 'runbook', 'Fix and rerun', 3, 65, 'for the load_date. Each rerun gets a new job id and reconciliation id. ## Verify Run the reconciliation and expect MATCH. Then check that the promotion appears in `gold_promo_performance` with a non-null uplift_pct. ## Escalation Raise a ticket in the data-engineering queue with the job id and the reject file. Promotion data belongs to the commercial team, so copy the promotion owner on the ticket.'),
  ('RB-SELL-IN-001#c1', 'RB-SELL-IN-001', 'Runbook - sell_in load job failure', 'sell_in', 'runbook', 'Runbook: sell_in load job failure', 1, 200, '# Runbook: sell_in load job failure ## Purpose and schedule This runbook covers failures of the pipeline `pl_fizzco_sell_in_load`, which loads sell-in orders (what Fizzco invoiced to retailers). The pipeline copies the file `sell_in_<load_date>.csv` from the SFTP source into the `fizzco-landing` container (folder `raw/sell_in/`) and then runs the notebook `/Fizzco/sell_in_transform` to build the trusted table `trusted_sell_in`. The trusted table adds one column, `net_amount`, which is `gross_amount` minus `discount_amount`. The pipeline runs once a day and takes the parameter `load_date`. ## Symptoms A sell_in job failure shows up in one of three ways: the pipeline run is marked Failed in the pipeline monitor; the pipeline succeeds but the reconciliation reports MISMATCH; or the trusted row count is lower than the raw row count. Treat all three as a sell_in failure and follow the steps below. ## Triage steps 1. Note the job id (format `JOB-####`) and the load_date of the failed run. 2. Open the failed activity. If `Copy_sell_in_to_raw` failed, the source file is the problem. If `Run_sell_in_transform` failed, the notebook is the problem. 3. Check the parameter `load_date`. It has no default value, so a manual trigger without it fails at the start of the run. Re-trigger with the date in'),
  ('RB-SELL-IN-001#c2', 'RB-SELL-IN-001', 'Runbook - sell_in load job failure', 'sell_in', 'runbook', 'Triage steps', 2, 200, 'file is the problem. If `Run_sell_in_transform` failed, the notebook is the problem. 3. Check the parameter `load_date`. It has no default value, so a manual trigger without it fails at the start of the run. Re-trigger with the date in the format YYYY-MM-DD. 4. If both activities succeeded, run the reconciliation: `python3 databricks/recon_utility.py` from the lake folder. A MISMATCH with a small difference usually points to the date window, not to lost data (see the cause list). ## Common causes specific to sell_in - **Missing source file.** The SFTP folder has no file for the load_date. Ask the sell-in owner to resend the file, then rerun the pipeline. Do not create an empty file. - **Empty load_date.** The run fails immediately. Rerun with load_date set. - **End-date filter error.** Reconciliation shows MISMATCH and the rows missing from the trusted count all carry the last day of the window, for example 31 March. Raw and trusted both hold every row; the counting code excluded the end date. This happened on 31 March 2026 (JOB-0049, RECON-0009). The fix is to make the end date inclusive (`<=`, not `<`). See the incident note for sell_in dated 2026-03-31. - **Bad discount value.** A row'),
  ('RB-SELL-IN-001#c3', 'RB-SELL-IN-001', 'Runbook - sell_in load job failure', 'sell_in', 'runbook', 'Common causes specific to sell_in', 3, 200, 'counting code excluded the end date. This happened on 31 March 2026 (JOB-0049, RECON-0009). The fix is to make the end date inclusive (`<=`, not `<`). See the incident note for sell_in dated 2026-03-31. - **Bad discount value.** A row where `discount_amount` is larger than `gross_amount` is rejected by the transform and written to the reject file. Send the reject file to the sell-in owner. ## Fix and rerun Fix the cause first, then rerun the pipeline with the same load_date. A rerun overwrites the trusted rows for that load_date, so it is safe to repeat. After the rerun, run the reconciliation again. Every rerun gets a new job id and a new reconciliation id; write the explanation in the `note` column, for example "rerun after the end-date fix". ## Verify The job is fixed only when the reconciliation shows status MATCH and diff_rows 0 for the same cut-off date. A green pipeline run alone is not enough. ## Escalation If the failure is not explained by the causes above, or if the reconciliation still shows MISMATCH after one rerun, raise a ticket in the data-engineering queue with the job id, the load_date, the failed activity name and the reconciliation'),
  ('RB-SELL-IN-001#c4', 'RB-SELL-IN-001', 'Runbook - sell_in load job failure', 'sell_in', 'runbook', 'Escalation', 4, 54, 'If the failure is not explained by the causes above, or if the reconciliation still shows MISMATCH after one rerun, raise a ticket in the data-engineering queue with the job id, the load_date, the failed activity name and the reconciliation output. Do not delete rows from the trusted table to make the counts agree.'),
  ('RB-SELL-OUT-001#c1', 'RB-SELL-OUT-001', 'Runbook - sell_out load job failure', 'sell_out', 'runbook', 'Runbook: sell_out load job failure', 1, 200, '# Runbook: sell_out load job failure ## Purpose and schedule This runbook covers failures of the pipeline `pl_fizzco_sell_out_load`, which loads sell-out data: units and value that retailers sold to shoppers, taken from retailer scan files. The pipeline copies `sell_out_<load_date>.csv` into the `fizzco-landing` container (folder `raw/sell_out/`) and runs the notebook `/Fizzco/sell_out_transform` to build `trusted_sell_out` (columns: sale_date, retailer_id, sku, units, sale_amount). Sell-out is the largest feed: the trusted table has more rows than any other feed. The pipeline runs once a day and takes the parameter `load_date`. ## Symptoms A sell_out job failure shows up as a Failed pipeline run, a reconciliation MISMATCH, or a retailer that has no sell-out rows for a week in which it had sell-in. ## Triage steps 1. Note the job id (format `JOB-####`) and the load_date. 2. Open the failed activity. `Copy_sell_out_to_raw` failing means a source file problem; `Run_sell_out_transform` failing means a notebook problem. 3. Check which retailers are in the file. Every active retailer (R001 to R010) should appear at least once per week. A missing retailer means a late scan file, not a pipeline fault. 4. If both activities succeeded, run the reconciliation and compare source and trusted rows. ## Common causes specific to'),
  ('RB-SELL-OUT-001#c2', 'RB-SELL-OUT-001', 'Runbook - sell_out load job failure', 'sell_out', 'runbook', 'Triage steps', 2, 200, 'to R010) should appear at least once per week. A missing retailer means a late scan file, not a pipeline fault. 4. If both activities succeeded, run the reconciliation and compare source and trusted rows. ## Common causes specific to sell_out - **Late retailer scan file.** One retailer sends its file after the daily run. The pipeline succeeds but that retailer is absent. Wait for the file, then rerun with the same load_date. - **Duplicate scan lines.** The same sale_date, retailer_id and sku appears twice in the raw file. The transform keeps one line and writes the duplicates to the reject file, so the trusted count is lower than the raw count by the number of duplicates. This is expected; record the number in the reconciliation note. - **Negative units.** Returns are sent as negative units by some retailers. The transform rejects negative units. Ask the retailer owner whether the return should be sent through the returns process instead. - **Unknown sku.** A sku that is not in the product dimension is rejected. Add the sku to the product dimension first, then rerun. ## Fix and rerun Fix the cause, then rerun the pipeline for the same load_date. The sell_out'),
  ('RB-SELL-OUT-001#c3', 'RB-SELL-OUT-001', 'Runbook - sell_out load job failure', 'sell_out', 'runbook', 'Common causes specific to sell_out', 3, 127, '**Unknown sku.** A sku that is not in the product dimension is rejected. Add the sku to the product dimension first, then rerun. ## Fix and rerun Fix the cause, then rerun the pipeline for the same load_date. The sell_out rerun is idempotent: it replaces the trusted rows for the load_date. Each rerun gets a new job id and reconciliation id. ## Verify Run the reconciliation. The expected status is MATCH with diff_rows 0, or a documented difference that equals the rejected duplicates. A difference with no explanation is a MISMATCH and must be escalated. ## Escalation Raise a ticket in the data-engineering queue with the job id, the load_date and the list of retailers missing from the file. Do not edit the trusted table by hand.'),
  ('LIN-INVENTORY#c1', 'LIN-INVENTORY', 'Lineage: pl_fizzco_inventory_load', 'inventory', 'lineage', 'Introduction', 1, 200, '> **AI-generated document. All data is synthetic. A human must review it before it is committed.** # Lineage: pl_fizzco_inventory_load ## Purpose Inventory snapshots - SFTP to raw, then raw to trusted (synthetic) ## Parameters | Name | Type | Default value | |---|---|---| | load_date | String | Not specified | ## Activity flow | Order | Activity name | Type | Depends on | Source | Target | |---|---|---|---|---|---| | 1 | Copy_inventory_to_raw | Copy | None | ds_sftp_fizzco_inventory | ds_adls_fizzco_raw_inventory (container fizzco-landing, folder raw/inventory/) | | 2 | Run_inventory_transform | DatabricksNotebook | Copy_inventory_to_raw (Succeeded) | Not specified (notebook parameter container = fizzco-landing) | Not specified | | 3 | Archive_Landing_Files | Copy | Run_inventory_transform (Succeeded) | Not specified | container fizzco-raw-archive, folder inventory/ | ## Lineage | Layer | Container | Folder | Evidence activity | |---|---|---|---| | Source | Not specified (dataset ds_sftp_fizzco_inventory) | Not specified | Copy_inventory_to_raw | | Raw | fizzco-landing | raw/inventory/ | Copy_inventory_to_raw | | Trusted | Not specified | Not specified | Run_inventory_transform | | Archive | fizzco-raw-archive | inventory/ | Archive_Landing_Files | ds_sftp_fizzco_inventory -> fizzco-landing/raw/inventory/ -> Not specified (trusted, via notebook /Fizzco/inventory_transform) ; archive: fizzco-raw-archive/inventory/ ## Notebook used - Path: /Fizzco/inventory_transform'),
  ('LIN-INVENTORY#c2', 'LIN-INVENTORY', 'Lineage: pl_fizzco_inventory_load', 'inventory', 'lineage', 'Lineage', 2, 134, '| | Trusted | Not specified | Not specified | Run_inventory_transform | | Archive | fizzco-raw-archive | inventory/ | Archive_Landing_Files | ds_sftp_fizzco_inventory -> fizzco-landing/raw/inventory/ -> Not specified (trusted, via notebook /Fizzco/inventory_transform) ; archive: fizzco-raw-archive/inventory/ ## Notebook used - Path: /Fizzco/inventory_transform - Parameters: - container = fizzco-landing - load_date = @pipeline().parameters.load_date ## Risks or observations - Copy_inventory_to_raw: container `fizzco-landing` and folder `raw/inventory/` are hard-coded; the folder does not use load_date. - Run_inventory_transform: container `fizzco-landing` and notebook path are hard-coded; trusted target not specified. - Archive_Landing_Files: no inputs or outputs dataset references; source is not specified. Container `fizzco-raw-archive` and folder `inventory/` are hard-coded. - All activities: no retry or timeout policy specified. - No failure-path (Failed/Completed) dependency handling and no alerting activity. - Parameter load_date has no default value. - No secrets found in the JSON.'),
  ('LIN-SELL-IN#c1', 'LIN-SELL-IN', 'Lineage: pl_fizzco_sell_in_load', 'sell_in', 'lineage', 'Introduction', 1, 200, '> **AI-generated document. All data is synthetic. A human must review it before it is committed.** # Lineage: pl_fizzco_sell_in_load ## Purpose Sell-in orders - SFTP to raw, then raw to trusted (synthetic) ## Parameters | Name | Type | Default value | |---|---|---| | load_date | String | Not specified | ## Activity flow | Order | Activity name | Type | Depends on | Source | Target | |---|---|---|---|---|---| | 1 | Copy_sell_in_to_raw | Copy | None | ds_sftp_fizzco_sell_in | ds_adls_fizzco_raw_sell_in (container fizzco-landing, folder raw/sell_in/) | | 2 | Run_sell_in_transform | DatabricksNotebook | Copy_sell_in_to_raw (Succeeded) | Not specified (notebook parameter container = fizzco-landing) | Not specified | ## Lineage | Layer | Container | Folder | Evidence activity | |---|---|---|---| | Source | Not specified (dataset ds_sftp_fizzco_sell_in) | Not specified | Copy_sell_in_to_raw | | Raw | fizzco-landing | raw/sell_in/ | Copy_sell_in_to_raw | | Trusted | Not specified | Not specified | Run_sell_in_transform | ds_sftp_fizzco_sell_in -> fizzco-landing/raw/sell_in/ -> Not specified (trusted, via notebook /Fizzco/sell_in_transform) ## Notebook used - Path: /Fizzco/sell_in_transform - Parameters: - container = fizzco-landing - load_date = @pipeline().parameters.load_date ## Risks or observations - Copy_sell_in_to_raw: container `fizzco-landing` and folder `raw/sell_in/` are hard-coded; the folder does not use load_date. -'),
  ('LIN-SELL-IN#c2', 'LIN-SELL-IN', 'Lineage: pl_fizzco_sell_in_load', 'sell_in', 'lineage', 'Lineage', 2, 92, '(trusted, via notebook /Fizzco/sell_in_transform) ## Notebook used - Path: /Fizzco/sell_in_transform - Parameters: - container = fizzco-landing - load_date = @pipeline().parameters.load_date ## Risks or observations - Copy_sell_in_to_raw: container `fizzco-landing` and folder `raw/sell_in/` are hard-coded; the folder does not use load_date. - Run_sell_in_transform: container `fizzco-landing` and notebook path are hard-coded. - Run_sell_in_transform: trusted target is not specified in the pipeline JSON. - Both activities: no retry or timeout policy specified. - No failure-path (Failed/Completed) dependency handling and no alerting activity. - Parameter load_date has no default value. - No secrets found in the JSON.');
